# Cronomicon runner agent env — copy to /etc/cronomicon-runner/runner.env (mode 0640,
# owned by the cronomicon-runner user) and fill in. Every var below is read by
# internal/agent/config.go; see documentation/runner-install.html (served in-app
# at /runner-install.html) for the worked examples.
#
# deploy/runner-install.sh can write this file for you: its --known-hosts,
# --key-dir / --key-map, --ca-cert, --inventory, and --local-inventory flags
# install the referenced files into the standard paths below and set the
# matching vars — no post-install hand-edit needed.
#
# Precedence (low → high): config file < these env vars < command-line flags.
# Leave a var unset to take the agent's built-in default (shown in comments).

# ── Server connection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base URL of the Cronomicon server (required). No trailing slash needed.
CRONOMICON_RUNNER_SERVER=https://cronomicon.example.com
# Optional PEM CA bundle to trust for TLS. Unset ⇒ system trust store.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_CA_CERT=/etc/cronomicon-runner/ca.pem

# ── Registration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The credential this runner presents at FIRST CONTACT to mint its long-lived
# API key, which is then persisted to the identity file. Two postures:
#
#   1. Single-use token (default) — an crn_reg_* minted for THIS install from the
#      Runners view (one per runner; 24h expiry). Spent by the first successful
#      registration. NOTE: if this runner ever loses its identity — the server no
#      longer has its row after a restore, or it is deregistered — it discards its
#      key and tries to register again, and a spent token FAILS (`token_used`).
#      The runner then stays offline until an operator mints a new token, puts it
#      here, and restarts the unit. Plan for that, or use posture 2.
#
#   2. Bootstrap token — the value of the server's CRONOMICON_RUNNER_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN.
#      Multi-use and never consumed, so re-enrollment completes unattended. It is
#      a PERMANENT credential: prefer supplying it through the agent's JSON config
#      file ({"registrationToken": "..."} via -config) rather than this env file,
#      because a job may name an environment variable in its env_passthrough and a
#      value that never enters the process environment cannot be reached that way.
#
# Either way, install it with `runner-install.sh --token-file <path>` or
# `--token -` rather than an inline `-t` value, which is visible in ps(1) and
# shell history.
CRONOMICON_RUNNER_REGISTRATION_TOKEN=crn_reg_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ── Identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Display name — MUST be stable across restarts so the identity file keeps
# pairing to the same runners row (required).
CRONOMICON_RUNNER_NAME=runner-dc1-01
# "Linux" (default) or "Windows".
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_OS=Linux
# Where {id, apiKey} is persisted (0600). Restart resumes this identity (R2.4).
CRONOMICON_RUNNER_IDENTITY_FILE=/var/lib/cronomicon-runner/identity.json

# ── Work selection ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated run-type capabilities. Unset (the recommended default) ⇒
# auto-detect: the agent probes the host's toolchains at startup and claims the
# matching run-types — install a toolchain later and a restart propagates it.
# Set explicitly only to NARROW what this runner claims (e.g. the host has
# python but must not run python jobs). Only matching runs are claimed.
# SSH-onward (slim image): bash,perl,powershell,python. Local toolchains (fat
# image): ansible,terraform.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_CAPABILITIES=bash,perl,powershell
# Max simultaneously-executing runs (default 5).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_MAX_CONCURRENT=5
# Inventory canonicality (D8): "cronomicon" (default — server resolves targets) or
# "local" (agent resolves the scope against its own inventory; T-b isolated).
CRONOMICON_RUNNER_INVENTORY=cronomicon

# ── Poll cadence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Heartbeat/poll cadence (default 60s — matches the server reaper's 5m offline
# threshold). The agent keeps polling while executing (heartbeat + kill/drain).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_POLL_INTERVAL=60s

# ── Key custody (credential model b — the agent holds its OWN keys) ────────────
# Cronomicon NEVER ships key bytes. A target's authKeyEnvVar is the NAME of the key
# the agent resolves locally, in this order:
#   1. key-map: NAME=path,NAME2=path2
#   2. an env var of that NAME holding a PEM (Go-SSH runs only — see below)
#   3. a file named <NAME>(.pem|.key) in key-dir
# ONE provisioned key file serves BOTH bash/Go-SSH AND ansible runs: the auth
# bridge resolves the authKeyEnvVar NAME to its key-map/key-dir FILE and hands
# ansible the path automatically — you no longer provision the same name twice
# (a key file for bash + an env-var-holding-a-path for ansible). NOTE the form 2
# env-PEM is NOT bridged to ansible (a PEM is bytes, not a path); use key-map or
# key-dir (form 1 or 3) for names that ansible must also use.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_KEY_MAP=PROD_DEPLOY_KEY=/var/lib/cronomicon-runner/keys/prod.pem
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_KEY_DIR=/var/lib/cronomicon-runner/keys
# OpenSSH known_hosts for strict host-key verification of TARGET hops. Without
# it the agent REFUSES to connect (no trust-on-first-use fall-open). The auth
# bridge ALSO points ansible's OpenSSH at THIS file (via ANSIBLE_SSH_COMMON_ARGS),
# so Scan & approve covers ansible too — one trust store for every run type (the
# old ~/.ssh/known_hosts symlink workaround is no longer needed).
CRONOMICON_RUNNER_KNOWN_HOSTS=/var/lib/cronomicon-runner/known_hosts

# ── Auth bridge (Phase 1 — key-name→file + known_hosts unification) ────────────
# Override the ANSIBLE_SSH_COMMON_ARGS the bridge synthesizes, for ProxyJump /
# cipher-restricted estates that must own the whole arg string. When set it is
# injected INSTEAD of the default "-o UserKnownHostsFile=<known_hosts> -o
# StrictHostKeyChecking=yes" (still fail-closed — do NOT add
# StrictHostKeyChecking=no). An operator value already in the child env
# (manifest env / env-base-extra / passthrough) is never overridden either way.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_ANSIBLE_SSH_COMMON_ARGS=-o ProxyJump=bastion -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes
# Escape hatch: disable the whole bridge (key-name→file resolution for ansible
# AND known_hosts injection). Runs then behave pre-Phase-1: the inventory must
# hand-author ansible_ssh_private_key_file and its own known_hosts. Default off.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_NO_AUTH_BRIDGE=false

# ── Local inventory (only when CRONOMICON_RUNNER_INVENTORY=local) ─────────────────
# JSON file mapping scope name → host targets the agent (and only the agent) can
# reach. Required in "local" mode. See install-runner.md for the format.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_LOCAL_INVENTORY=/etc/cronomicon-runner/inventory.json

# ── Tuning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Max log-POST resume attempts before the run lands log_stream_lost (default 5).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_LOG_RETRY_BUDGET=5
# Max parallel SSH targets within a single run (default 4).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_FAN_OUT=4

# ── File-arrival triggers (opt-in — ET-D) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Opt into watching directories so a job runs when a file lands. Off by default:
# a watching agent reads a filesystem on the SERVER's instruction, so it has to
# be an explicit local choice.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_ALLOW_WATCH=true
# Directory roots this runner may watch (comma-separated). The server supplies
# the globs; this is the local bound on them, and it is what stops a careless or
# compromised server pointing a runner at a sensitive directory. An EMPTY list
# permits nothing — setting ALLOW_WATCH without this watches no files at all.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_WATCH_PATHS=/srv/incoming,/var/spool/cronomicon

# ── Ansible checkout projects (opt-in — see runner-install.html §12) ───────────
# Opt into runner-side project checkout (roles/vars_files/.j2 at a pinned SHA).
# Without it a checkout run is refused. Requires an ansible-capable fat runner.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_ALLOW_CHECKOUT=true
# Allowlist of repo clone URLs this runner may check out (comma-separated). A
# checkout repo outside this list is refused.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_CHECKOUT_REPOS=https://gitlab.example.com/infra/playbooks.git
# Read-only deploy token (read_repository only), NEVER shipped by the server.
# Prefer the file form. A bare token uses oauth2:<token>; a user:token value is
# used verbatim (deploy-token form). runner-install.sh installs this file for you
# (--checkout-token-file <path>, or --checkout-token - to type it at a hidden
# prompt) at 0640 root:cronomicon-runner — no manual placement needed.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_CHECKOUT_TOKEN_FILE=/etc/cronomicon-runner/checkout-token
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_CHECKOUT_TOKEN=glpat-xxxxxxxxxxxx
# Persistent bare-mirror dir (default <state-dir>/mirrors).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_MIRROR_DIR=/var/lib/cronomicon-runner/mirrors

# ── Galaxy / requirements.yml ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ansible-galaxy source for per-run requirements installs. Unset ⇒ public
# galaxy.ansible.com; point at a private mirror/hub to stay off the internet.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_GALAXY_SERVER=https://hub.example.com/api/galaxy/content/published/

# ── Ansible Vault (opt-in) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runner-local Vault password file. A job that requires:[vault] is passed
# --vault-password-file <this>; the password NEVER travels. Also register this
# password as an Cronomicon stored secret so the log redactor masks it. Values a
# playbook DECRYPTS from vault bypass redaction — gate them with no_log: true.
# runner-install.sh installs this file too (--vault-pass-file <path>, or
# --vault-pass - to type it at a hidden prompt) at 0640 root:cronomicon-runner.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_VAULT_PASSWORD_FILE=/etc/cronomicon-runner/vault-pass

# ── Runner-local secrets (become/WinRM passwords, etc.) ───────────────────────
# Values a play resolves by NAME via {{ lookup('env','NAME') }} (e.g.
# ansible_become_password) are read from the agent's OWN environment — put them
# in a root:cronomicon-runner 0640 file and source it. This unit sources an OPTIONAL
# /etc/cronomicon-runner/secrets.env (EnvironmentFile=-, a no-op when absent), so a
# hand-managed file works out of the box:
#   # /etc/cronomicon-runner/secrets.env
#   NWD_BECOME_PASS=...
# To make Vault the source of truth instead of hand-editing that file, install
# the opt-in Vault Agent sidecar, if you run one on the host, which renders the same
# secrets.env from a KV mount under the runner's own AppRole. Cronomicon stays
# names-only (D1): the server never sees these values. Restart the runner to pick
# up a rotated secret.

# ── Scoped child env (always-on for local-toolchain runs) ─────────────────────
# Extra env-var NAMES (beyond the base safe-list) forwarded to a playbook's
# scoped environment. Names only — the agent resolves values from its own env.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_ENV_BASE_EXTRA=ANSIBLE_CONFIG,HTTPS_PROXY
# Drop SSH_AUTH_SOCK from the base safe-list (for runners using per-host key
# files instead of an ssh-agent).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_EXCLUDE_SSH_AUTH_SOCK=false

# ── Tier 2 sandbox (per-run systemd-run scope; Linux) ─────────────────────────
# Availability is probed at startup; where unavailable, runs execute UNSANDBOXED
# and are reported as such. Set to disable the wrapper entirely.
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_NO_SANDBOX=false
# Per-run cgroup resource caps (empty ⇒ that dimension uncapped).
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_SANDBOX_MEMORY_MAX=2G
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_SANDBOX_CPU_QUOTA=150%
# CRONOMICON_RUNNER_SANDBOX_TASKS_MAX=512
